Today, Lieutenant Governor Randy McNally shared that he will not seek re-election due to health concerns. With that announcement, our state marks the close of an extraordinary era of public service.

First elected to the General Assembly in 1978, Lt. Governor McNally has devoted nearly half a century to the people of Tennessee. Few public servants can point to a career that has spanned so many chapters of our state’s history. Through political shifts, institutional change, and personal challenges, he remained a constant presence in this chamber and a steadfast advocate for the institution he helped shape.

He also played a pivotal role during one of the most difficult periods in our legislature’s history, the fallout from Operation Tennessee Waltz. In the wake of that corruption scandal, when public trust in state government had been shaken, he was part of the effort to restore integrity, strengthen ethics reforms, and help rebuild confidence in this body. That moment required steady leadership and a willingness to confront hard truths about our institution, and he helped guide the Senate through it.

As Speaker of the Senate, he led in a way that reflected his temperament: steady, measured, and grounded in a deep respect for the traditions of this body.

We have not always seen eye to eye. That is the nature of a deliberative body, and the work we do carries real consequences for the people we serve. But disagreement need not erode respect. I have always found him to be thoughtful in debate and mindful of the responsibility entrusted to him by Tennesseans. At a moment when our nation feels so deeply divided, it is bittersweet to see true conservatives, those who value civility, mutual respect, and the hard work of compromise,step away from public life, because that kind of steady leadership is needed now more than ever.

In my time in the Senate, he has extended courtesy and professionalism to me and to members across the aisle. That spirit matters. Institutions endure when those who lead them recognize that service is greater than any single moment or issue.

Public service demands sacrifice,not only from those elected, but from their families as well. I extend my appreciation to Lt. Governor McNally, to his wife Janice, and to their family for the years they have dedicated to this state.

As he steps into retirement, I wish him improved health, well-earned rest, and meaningful time with those he loves. Tennessee is stronger because of his decades of commitment to this chamber and to the people we represent.

Heidi

Senator Heidi Campbell is a Tennessee State Senator, representing District 20. She is championing change for Tennessee and won't rest until we see gun safety reform, corporate accountability, and a safe and prosperous community where everyone can thrive.
